      <Desc>MENDING MATTERS PROVIDED DIRECT MENTAL HEALTH SERVICES TO MORE THAN 1,300 STUDENTS ACROSS MIDDLE AND HIGH SCHOOLS, DELIVERING OVER 3,500 INDIVIDUAL, GROUP, AND CRISIS SERVICES. STUDENTS ENGAGED IN 8-10 SESSION TREATMENT CYCLES, WITH AN AVERAGE 82-84% COMPLETION OF TREATMENT GOALS, MEETING BENCHMARKS FOR EFFECTIVENESS.THROUGH YOUTH ENGAGEMENT AND EMPOWERMENT PROGRAMS, OVER 10,000 STUDENTS PARTICIPATED IN COMMUNITY EVENTS, CLASSROOM PRESENTATIONS, AND SURVEYS, INCLUDING 7,417 WHO ACCESSED MENTAL HEALTH AWARENESS CONTENT. THESE PROGRAMS INCREASED STUDENT KNOWLEDGE OF AVAILABLE SUPPORTS, WITH OVER 90% OF PARTICIPANTS REPORTING GREATER ABILITY TO MANAGE CHALLENGES AND IDENTIFY RESOURCES.THE PAUSE PROGRAM DELIVERED PREVENTION WORKSHOPS TO MORE THAN 1,400 STUDENTS, FOCUSING ON STRESS MANAGEMENT, BOUNDARIES, AND COMMUNICATION. POST-WORKSHOP SURVEYS SHOWED THAT 91% OF YOUTH LEARNED STRATEGIES TO HANDLE DIFFICULT SITUATIONS AND 96% KNEW HOW TO ACCESS SUPPORT. THE LIFE STUDENT LEADERSHIP PROGRAM TRAINED NEARLY 100 YOUTH ADVISORS ACROSS SCHOOLS, PROVIDING LEADERSHIP DEVELOPMENT AND PEER SUPPORT OPPORTUNITIES THAT STRENGTHENED STUDENT VOICE AND ENGAGEMENT.FAMILY INCLUSION REMAINED A CORE COMPONENT, WITH MORE THAN 200 PARENT/GUARDIAN ENGAGEMENT MEETINGS AND WORKSHOPS CONDUCTED, INCREASING FAMILY AWARENESS OF STUDENT NEEDS AND AVAILABLE RESOURCES.</Desc>
